<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hall of Mirrors</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hall of Mirrors is the grand gallery of the Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. In January 1871, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> there. The choice of a French royal palace for the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> was a deliberate symbol of victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors.jpg" alt="Hall of Mirrors" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Myrabella · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chateau_Versailles_Galerie_des_Glaces.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
